<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Noun and Pronoun Agreement - Tricky cases</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>Everyone, somebody, anyone and each</h2>
    <p>These words are singular, even when they seem to mean a group.</p>
    <p>
      Everyone did <strong>his or her</strong> homework.
      <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      Everyone did <strong>their</strong> homework.
      <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
      <em>Their</em> is too informal for GL here.
    </p>
    <p>Each of the boys brought <strong>his</strong> lunch. The head word is <em>each</em>, not <em>boys</em>.</p>
    <p>Somebody left <strong>his or her</strong> umbrella.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Do not change number halfway</h2>
    <p>
      If a person wants to win, <strong>they</strong> must train hard.
      <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      If a person wants to win, <strong>he or she</strong> must train hard.
      <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>A group, or the people in it</h2>
    <p>The team lost <strong>its</strong> first game. The team is one group.</p>
    <p>The team took <strong>their</strong> seats. Here the pronoun means the members.</p>
    <p>With <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, the pronoun usually agrees with the nearer noun. Neither Tom nor Jack brought <strong>his</strong> shoes.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>Make it clear who you mean</h2>
    <p>When John met Tom, <strong>he</strong> said hello. <em>He</em> could be John or Tom.</p>
    <p>Name the person, or rewrite the sentence, so the pronoun has one clear antecedent.</p>
  </section>
</div>
